        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Logistic Regression\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression is a classification method for binary outcomes. It models the conditional probability of one class as a function of the predictors.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a binary response, write\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal is to estimate\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Prob(Y=1\\mid X=x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This probability can then be used for two tasks:\n",
        "\n",
        "- probability estimation,\n",
        "- classification by thresholding the probability.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Modeling Binary Outcomes\n",
        "\n",
        "In linear regression, we model a quantitative response by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y=\\beta_0+\\beta_1x_1+\\cdots+\\beta_px_p+\\epsilon.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is not appropriate for a binary response. A linear model can produce fitted values below 0 or above 1, so its fitted values cannot always be interpreted as probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression instead models\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\Prob(Y=1\\mid X=x)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "using the logistic function:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\n",
        "\\exp(\\beta_0+\\beta_1x_1+\\cdots+\\beta_px_p)\n",
        "}{\n",
        "1+\\exp(\\beta_0+\\beta_1x_1+\\cdots+\\beta_px_p)\n",
        "}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\eta(x)=\\beta_0+\\beta_1x_1+\\cdots+\\beta_px_p.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi(x)=\\frac{e^{\\eta(x)}}{1+e^{\\eta(x)}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This always lies between 0 and 1.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Main idea\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression is linear in the log-odds, not in the probability.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Odds and the Logit Link\n",
        "\n",
        "The odds of class 1 are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\pi(x)}{1-\\pi(x)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For logistic regression,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1-\\pi(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{1+e^{\\eta(x)}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\pi(x)}{1-\\pi(x)}\n",
        "=\n",
        "e^{\\eta(x)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Taking logs gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\left(\n",
        "\\frac{\\pi(x)}{1-\\pi(x)}\n",
        "\\right)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\eta(x)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\beta_0+\\beta_1x_1+\\cdots+\\beta_px_p.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The function\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\left(\\frac{p}{1-p}\\right)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is called the logit function. Logistic regression is a generalized linear model with a Bernoulli response and logit link.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Interpreting Coefficients\n",
        "\n",
        "The coefficients are interpreted through odds ratios.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose all predictors except $x_j$ are fixed. If $x_j$ increases by one unit, then the log-odds increase by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\beta_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Exponentiating gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{odds ratio}=e^{\\beta_j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $\\beta_j$ is the change in log-odds for a one-unit increase in $x_j$;\n",
        "- $e^{\\beta_j}$ is the multiplicative change in the odds.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-caution}\n",
        "## Not a probability change\n",
        "\n",
        "A logistic regression coefficient is not a direct change in probability. The probability change depends on the current value of all predictors.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, if $\\beta_j=0.05$, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e^{0.05}\\approx 1.051.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Holding other predictors fixed, a one-unit increase in $x_j$ multiplies the odds by about $1.051$, or increases the odds by about $5.1\\%$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## From Probability to Classification\n",
        "\n",
        "After fitting the model, the estimated probability is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat\\pi(x)=\\widehat{\\Prob}(Y=1\\mid X=x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A common classification rule is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat Y\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "1, & \\hat\\pi(x)>0.5,\\\\\n",
        "0, & \\hat\\pi(x)\\le 0.5.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The threshold does not have to be 0.5. If false positives and false negatives have different costs, the threshold should reflect those costs.\n",
        "\n",
        "## A One-Variable Calculation\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the fitted model is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\left(\\frac{\\pi(x)}{1-\\pi(x)}\\right)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-3.006+0.052x.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a subject with $x=55$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\eta(55)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-3.006+0.052(55)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-0.146.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The estimated probability is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi(55)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{e^{-0.146}}{1+e^{-0.146}}\n",
        "\\approx 0.463.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "So the model estimates a class-1 probability of about $46.3\\%$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Estimation by Maximum Likelihood\n",
        "\n",
        "For observation $i$, let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi_i=\\pi(x_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $Y_i$ is binary,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y_i\\mid X_i=x_i \\sim \\operatorname{Bernoulli}(\\pi_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The probability mass function is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Prob(Y_i=y_i\\mid X_i=x_i)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\pi_i^{y_i}(1-\\pi_i)^{1-y_i}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For independent observations, the likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(\\beta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{i=1}^n\n",
        "\\pi_i^{y_i}(1-\\pi_i)^{1-y_i}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\beta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "\\left[\n",
        "y_i\\log(\\pi_i)\n",
        "+\n",
        "(1-y_i)\\log(1-\\pi_i)\n",
        "\\right].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Using the logistic form, this can be written as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\beta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "\\left[\n",
        "y_i x_i^\\top\\beta\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\log\\{1+\\exp(x_i^\\top\\beta)\\}\n",
        "\\right].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression does not have the same closed-form solution as ordinary least squares. The coefficients are found by numerical optimization, commonly Newton-type methods.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Deviance\n",
        "\n",
        "In logistic regression and other generalized linear models, deviance measures lack of fit.\n",
        "\n",
        "Two common quantities are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- null deviance: deviance for an intercept-only model;\n",
        "- residual deviance: deviance for the fitted model.\n",
        "\n",
        "A large reduction from null deviance to residual deviance means the predictors explain useful information about the class probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Logistic Regression in `statsmodels`\n",
        "\n",
        "`statsmodels` is useful when the goal is classical statistical output: coefficient tables, standard errors, z-statistics, and p-values.\n",
        "\n",
        "The following example uses the breast cancer dataset from `sklearn`."
      ],
      "id": "187a909e"
